A||N||14||Haze_Performance||V6 ALTERNATOR REMOVAL!!!!!!!!!!||||8||
Z||000000||Haze_Performance||10-30-2000||04:14 AM||||'<BR>-------<BR>       <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/2d_00010aa3.gif">  <BR><B>Website's  <A HREF="http://www.WS6Fiero.com" TARGET=_blank>www.WS6Fiero.com</A>-                  <A HREF="http://www.hazeperformance.com" TARGET=_blank>www.HazePerformance.com </A></B><p>[This message has been edited by Haze_Performance (edited 04-02-2001).]||||reg||8
Z||000001||Haze_Performance||10-30-2000||04:19 AM||||P.S. I was looking at the tie rod & it doesnt look like it would matter if I removed it. The Alt. still hits the Drive Axle boot.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"> <P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/2d_00010aa3.gif"> <BR><B>Website's  <A HREF="http://www.WS6Fiero.com" TARGET=_blank>www.WS6Fiero.com</A>-                  <A HREF="http://www.hazeperformance.com" TARGET=_blank>www.HazePerformance.com </A></B>||||reg||11
Z||000002||Phil||10-30-2000||06:55 AM||||I took one out of my son's 88 GT - just did some twisting, turning and flipping and it came out. I didn't have to remove anything.||||reg||1
Z||000003||bHooper||10-30-2000||07:24 AM||||Carl, as I have swapped/removed, my altenator 6 times in the past 2 months... I consider myself somewhat of a expert in this matter.<P>The easist way is as follows:<P>1) have the rear on jackstands, high enough so that you can almost fully extend your arm to the top bolt, turn your body so that your legs are pointed towards the passenger tire.<P>2)remove all plugs.<P>3) most important, remove the heat shield <B>1st</B><P>4)pull the altenator towards the drivers side  (to seperate it from the bracket)<P>5)pull it straight down... there is a opening (i cant remember where, you will see it, now) that the alt. will fit through.<P>It really isnt that hard to do, you just need a little practice!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>hoop<BR>Red '86 GT||||reg||1
Z||000004||Indiana_resto_guy||10-30-2000||07:27 AM||||I had to remove the outer tie rod end and loosen the bracket. (I went on ahead and removed it to clean it). Seemed like a major pain in the A_ _, but reading about it was worse than doing it.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Haze_Performance||10-30-2000||03:25 PM||||Thanks Guys.... Ill give it a try again...<P>Ill let ya know how it go's..<P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/2d_00010aa3.gif"> <BR><B>Website's  <A HREF="http://www.WS6Fiero.com" TARGET=_blank>www.WS6Fiero.com</A>-                  <A HREF="http://www.hazeperformance.com" TARGET=_blank>www.HazePerformance.com </A></B>||||reg||14
Z||000006||william_thorniii||10-30-2000||03:44 PM||||Haze,<P>I hear you.  First and most importantly... step back take a deep breath and relax.  Now you are ready to go back and have at it.  <P>It took me forever to figure out how I could get mine out with the least disassembly.  I ended up jacking the car up and removing the rear passenger wheel.  After removing the pass. wheel well material, there was plenty of space.  The secret for me was to jack the car up, it increased the angle of the suspension to the centerline of the car and therefore increased the gap.  <P>Like you though, I spent 20 minutes unbolting and about 5 hours of pissing, moaning, groaning and cussing... I think I threw an wrench in there somewhere.  On a side note, if anyone happens to find a Craftsman 15mm open/box end wrench in Middletown, OH please return it to it's rightful owner :~)<P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||10
Z||000007||LAMBO||10-30-2000||04:31 PM||||Am I missing something here or what. Comes out the top in 15 min. From memory,<BR>1. Remove engine side dogbone bolt and flip dogbone up out of the way. <BR>2. Remove heat shield<BR>3. Remove heater hose from steel tube mounted on trunk wall. (might loose a little antifreeze, just quickly turn hose upward and wedge into something to hold it there.)<BR>4. Remove both alternator brackets. 5 bolts total. Its really not that big a deal.<BR>5. Remove plug wire closest to alternator<BR>6. Pull your alternator out the top. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR>TL<P><BR>Yes, its a pain, but what isn't on a Fiero.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Haze_Performance||10-30-2000||04:35 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by william_thorniii:<BR><B>Haze,<P>I hear you.  First and most importantly... step back take a deep breath and relax.  Now you are ready to go back and have at it.  <P>It took me forever to figure out how I could get mine out with the least disassembly.  I ended up jacking the car up and removing the rear passenger wheel.  After removing the pass. wheel well material, there was plenty of space.  The secret for me was to jack the car up, it increased the angle of the suspension to the centerline of the car and therefore increased the gap.  <P>Like you though, I spent 20 minutes unbolting and about 5 hours of pissing, moaning, groaning and cussing... I think I threw an wrench in there somewhere.  On a side note, if anyone happens to find a Craftsman 15mm open/box end wrench in Middletown, OH please return it to it's rightful owner :~)<P>William T. Thorn, III</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I have the car jacked up on the center of the frame. The Pass. wheel is off. The alt. is completely disconnected. All the rubber (or whatever it is) is removed from the well. Even with all this the alt. still wont clear the wheel well metal or the drive shaft.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"> <BR><P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/2d_00010aa3.gif"> <BR><B>Website's  <A HREF="http://www.WS6Fiero.com" TARGET=_blank>www.WS6Fiero.com</A>-                  <A HREF="http://www.hazeperformance.com" TARGET=_blank>www.HazePerformance.com </A></B>||||reg||9
Z||000009||Haze_Performance||10-30-2000||05:03 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by LAMBO:<BR><B>Am I missing something here or what. Comes out the top in 15 min. From memory,<BR>1. Remove engine side dogbone bolt and flip dogbone up out of the way. <BR>2. Remove heat shield<BR>3. Remove heater hose from steel tube mounted on trunk wall. (might loose a little antifreeze, just quickly turn hose upward and wedge into something to hold it there.)<BR>4. Remove both alternator brackets. 5 bolts total. Its really not that big a deal.<BR>5. Remove plug wire closest to alternator<BR>6. Pull your alternator out the top.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR>TL<P><BR>Yes, its a pain, but what isn't on a Fiero.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Sounds easy.. but not without removing the alt. bracket??<P><P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/2d_00010aa3.gif"> <BR><B>Website's  <A HREF="http://www.WS6Fiero.com" TARGET=_blank>www.WS6Fiero.com</A>-                  <A HREF="http://www.hazeperformance.com" TARGET=_blank>www.HazePerformance.com </A></B>||||reg||2
Z||000010||My7Fieros||10-30-2000||07:53 PM||||Haze, I did it on my 86 Gt..I didnt remove the alt bracket, just the alt. BUT THERE WAS NO WAY IN HELL THAT THAT DAMN THING WAS COMMING OUT EITHER THROUGH THE TOP OR THROUGH THE WHEEL WELL!!!!! <BR>I had to actually take the alt. apart in order for it to come out throught the wheel well!!!!!!!!!!!!!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/mad.gif"><BR>Second to replacing the foward exhaust manifold, it was the most frustrating thing Ive ever done on a Fiero!!!!!!!!!!!!!!<BR> ||||reg||1
Z||000011||Carrolles||10-30-2000||09:43 PM||||I just did one on my 85GT auto.  Remove the heater pipe attached to the rear firewall above the muffler, the dogbone and the dogbone bracket that is bolted to the rear head.  There is one bolt near the rear center spark plug, one on the right side of the head, and one attachnig it to the bracket going to the front head.  It's not all that hard to do.<P>Good luck,<P>Carroll||||reg||1
Z||000012||bHooper||10-31-2000||06:56 AM||||<B>YOU GUYS ARE ALL CRAZY, INSANE, MAD, I TELL YA!</B><P>Once you have it disconected, it drops out the bottom!  Very easy!.... Out the bottom.<P>The jackstands do need to be back on the cradle, near the control arms.<P>It has nothing to do with the tire well or the exhaust manifold, or the spare tire, the battery, the sunroof, the cooling pipes!!!!!!<P>It very simply drops out the bottom!<P>Carl, If you were closer, I would drop buy and do in 10 min!<P>------------------<BR>hoop<BR>Red '86 GT||||reg||1
Z||000013||robertjay||10-31-2000||07:54 AM||||I agree with Hoop, I have gotten quite skilled at removing my alternator lately.  First I had to replace it, then about a thousand miles later, the pulley came loose.  After I tightened it up and put it on, it had a serious vibration at 3000 rpm's.  So off it came again, ordered a new pulley from GM.  Then recently, I did my exhaust manifolds, so it came off again.  After it came loose, move it back to the center of the compartment and it comes right out the bottom.  I removed the air cooling pipe just to gain better access to it when removing the bolts.  Mine's an '85 GT.  I'm not sure if something is different about any other models.  Good Luck!||||reg||1
Z||000014||bHooper||10-31-2000||09:56 AM||||Exactly, it really is simple.  I didnt even have to remove the blower pipe.<P>The most anoying part of the whole job, is not having enough room to get full motion with the ratchet!  Other than that, it is as easy as changing chanels on the TV!<P>Tightening belts... now thats a different story....  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Carl, you can do... i know you can!<P>------------------<BR>hoop<BR>Red '86 GT||||reg||1
